1. Introduction
1.1 Purpose
The purpose of this document is to define the requirements of the University Library Management System. It describes the system's functionality, users, limitations, interfaces and quality requirements. This document will guide the future design and development of the system.
1.2 Scope
The University Library Management System is intended to help a university library manage its book catalog, book borrowing, returns, reservations, user accounts and overdue books.

The system will allow students and faculty members to search for books and check their availability. Librarians will manage book records, borrowing and returns, while administrators will manage user accounts and system access.

The academic project focuses on requirements analysis, system design, UML and UI prototyping. A complete production system is not required.
1.3 Definitions
- SRS: Software Requirements Specification.
- FR: Functional Requirement.
- NFR: Non-Functional Requirement.
- Stakeholder: A person or group affected by the system.
- Acceptance Criteria: Conditions used to determine whether a requirement or feature is satisfied.
- ISBN: International Standard Book Number used to identify books.
2. Overall System Description
2.1 Product Perspective
The University Library Management System is a proposed software solution for organizing university library operations. It aims to reduce manual work, improve book availability tracking and provide an organized way to manage library records.
2.2 Stakeholders
Stakeholder	Description
Student	Searches, checks availability and borrows or reserves books.
Faculty Member	Searches and borrows academic books.
Librarian	Manages books, borrowing, returns and reservations.
Library Administrator	Manages user accounts, permissions and library operations.


2.3 System Features
The system is expected to provide the following features:
- User login and access control.
- Book searching and availability checking.
- Book catalog management.
- Borrowing and return management.
- Due date management.
- Book reservation management.
- Overdue book identification.
- User account management.
- Borrowing history.
2.4 User Characteristics
- Students and faculty members will use the system to search and access library resources.
- Librarians will use management functions for daily library activities.
- Administrators will manage accounts, permissions and system operations.
Users are expected to have basic computer and internet usage skills.
3. Functional Requirements
FR-01 — User Login
The system shall allow registered students, faculty members, librarians and library administrators to log in using their credentials.
FR-02 — Book Search
The system shall allow users to search for books by title, author, ISBN or category.
FR-03 — Book Availability
The system shall display the availability status of each book, including whether available or currently borrowed.
FR-04 — Book Record Management
The system shall allow librarians to add, update and remove book records from the catalog.
FR-05 — Book Borrowing
The system shall allow librarians to issue available books to registered library members.
FR-06 — Book Return
The system shall allow librarians to record return of borrowed books.
FR-07 — Due Date Management
The system shall assign a due date to each borrowed book according to applicable borrowing policy.
FR-08 — Book Reservation
The system shall allow registered users to reserve books currently unavailable.
FR-09 — Reservation Management
The system shall allow librarians to view and manage book reservations.
FR-10 — Overdue Book Identification
The system shall identify borrowed books whose due dates have passed and mark them as overdue.
FR-11 — User Management
The system shall allow library administrators to create, update and manage library user accounts.
FR-12 — Borrowing History
The system shall allow library staff to view borrowing and return history of registered library members.
4. Non-Functional Requirements
NFR-01 — Performance
The system shall display search results within 3 seconds under normal operating conditions.
NFR-02 — Security
The system shall restrict access to administrative and librarian functions to authorized users.
NFR-03 — Usability
The system shall provide a user interface that allows users to access book search and availability information within a maximum of three navigation steps from the main dashboard.
NFR-04 — Reliability
The system shall maintain accurate borrowing, return and reservation records during normal operation.
NFR-05 — Compatibility
The system shall support access through the latest stable versions of Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge and Mozilla Firefox.
NFR-06 — Data Backup
The system shall support daily backups of library records to allow data recovery in case of data loss.
5. External Interface Requirements
5.1 User Interface
The system shall provide an interface that allows users to access features according to their assigned roles.

The proposed interface should include:
- Login page.
- Main dashboard.
- Book search page.
- Book availability page.
- Borrowing and return management page.
- Reservation management page.
- User management page.
5.2 Hardware Interface
The system is expected to be accessed through computers or laptops connected to the internet. No specialized hardware is required for the academic prototype.
5.3 Software Interface
The proposed system shall be accessible through supported web browsers. Specific backend technologies, database software and deployment tools have not yet been selected.
5.4 Communication Interface
The proposed system will require an internet connection for accessing online library services.
6. Assumptions, Constraints and Dependencies
6.1 Assumptions
- A-01: Students and faculty members will have valid university identification or registration details to access library services.
- A-02: The library will provide accurate book information, including titles, authors, ISBNs and available copies.
- A-03: The library will establish borrowing periods, renewal rules and reservation policies before implementation.
6.2 Constraints
- C-01: The system will require an internet connection for users to access online library services.
- C-02: The academic project is limited to requirements analysis, system design, UML and UI prototyping. A complete production system is not required.
- C-03: Students and faculty members will have limited access to library functions, while librarians and administrators will have management access according to their permissions.
6.3 Dependencies
- The system depends on accurate library catalog data.
- Borrowing and reservation functionality depends on confirmed library policies.
- User management depends on the availability of valid user registration information.
7. Acceptance Criteria
The system's requirements will be evaluated using the acceptance criteria documented in requirements/acceptance-criteria.md.

The major acceptance conditions include:
- Users can search for books using supported search fields.
- Book availability is displayed correctly.
- Librarians can issue and return books.
- Borrowing records contain borrowing and due dates.
- Registered users can reserve unavailable books.
- Librarians can manage book records and reservations.
- Administrators can manage user accounts and access permissions.
